Database migrations (Alembic via Flask-Migrate)
===============================================

The schema is owned by the migrations in versions/ -- NOT by db.create_all().
Adding a column to a model does nothing to a real database until a migration
exists and has been applied.

All commands run from backend/ with FLASK_APP set:

    export FLASK_APP=run.py

First-time setup, or after pulling changes that touch app/models/:

    ./venv/bin/flask db upgrade

After changing a model (new column, new table, changed constraint):

    ./venv/bin/flask db migrate -m "what changed"   # writes versions/<hash>_*.py
    # READ the generated file before applying it -- autogenerate misses some
    # things (notably CHECK constraint edits and column renames, which it
    # records as a drop plus an add, losing the data in that column).
    ./venv/bin/flask db upgrade

Useful checks:

    ./venv/bin/flask db current      # which revision this database is on
    ./venv/bin/flask db history      # all revisions
    ./venv/bin/flask db downgrade    # step back one revision
    ./venv/bin/flask db migrate -m x # if it says "No changes in schema
                                     # detected", the database matches the
                                     # models -- a handy drift check

Adopting an existing database
-----------------------------
A database that already has the tables (built with create_all() before
migrations existed) must be marked as current rather than upgraded, or
Alembic will try to CREATE tables that are already there:

    ./venv/bin/flask db stamp head

That is exactly how grading_system_dev was brought under Alembic: its schema
was verified column-for-column, constraint-for-constraint against a database
built from the baseline migration, then stamped.

Note on the test database
-------------------------
The test scripts still build their schema with db.create_all() for speed, so
they bypass migrations entirely. Those two paths are in sync as of the
baseline revision (verified by diffing information_schema), but they can drift:
if a migration is hand-edited so it no longer matches the models, the tests
will not catch it. Running `flask db upgrade` against a scratch database and
diffing is the way to confirm.
